# Trivy vulnerability waivers for the orchestration content image.
#
# kubectl (usr/local/bin/kubectl, orchestration/Dockerfile) is flagged for CVEs in the
# Go modules it is compiled against, not in kubectl's own code. v1.36.6, the latest patch
# on the EKS-pinned minor, is built on go1.26.10 but still ships golang.org/x/net v0.56.0
# and golang.org/x/text v0.39.0, so there is nothing to bump to for the CVEs below.
#
# CVE-2026-78669 (golang.org/x/net http2, fixed in 0.60.0): a malicious HTTP/2 peer can
# burn CPU in a client as well as a server, so the worker's kubectl is in reach. Its one
# peer is the AWS-managed EKS API endpoint, reached over TLS verified against the cluster
# CA, so exploiting it needs a compromised control plane or a broken TLS chain, and the
# impact is CPU exhaustion of the worker pod.
#
# CVE-2026-78661 (golang.org/x/net/html, fixed in 0.62.0) is a denial of service when
# parsing crafted HTML. kubectl v1.36.6 links golang.org/x/net/html, but the only input it
# could parse is a response from the EKS API, behind the same verified TLS as above. The
# Terraform tls provider (opt/terraform/plugins/.../tls/4.4.2, the latest release) is
# flagged for its x/net v0.61.0 but does not link golang.org/x/net/html: its binary holds
# no symbol from that package, so the flagged code is not in the image.
#
# CVE-2026-56851 (golang.org/x/text, fixed in 0.41.0) is a panic in the precis Nickname
# profile. kubectl v1.36.6 does not link golang.org/x/text/secure/precis: the binary holds
# no symbol from that package, so the flagged code is not in the image.
#
# Drop each line once upstream kubectl (or the tls provider) ships the patched module. The
# exp date forces the scan to fail again and this file to be revisited rather than rot
# silently.
CVE-2026-78669 exp:2026-12-09
CVE-2026-78661 exp:2026-12-09
CVE-2026-56851 exp:2026-12-09
